{{- $sdkName := (sanitizeClassName .Global.AST.MainSDK.Type.Name) -}}
The TypeScript SDK makes API calls using an `HTTPClient` that wraps the native
[Fetch API](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Fetch_API). This
client is a thin wrapper around `fetch` and provides the ability to attach hooks
around the request lifecycle that can be used to modify the request or handle
errors and response.

The `HTTPClient` constructor takes an optional `fetcher` argument that can be
used to integrate a third-party HTTP client or when writing tests to mock out
the HTTP client and feed in fixtures.

The following example shows how to:
- route requests through a proxy server using [undici](https://www.npmjs.com/package/undici)'s ProxyAgent
- use the `"beforeRequest"` hook to add a custom header and a timeout to requests
- use the `"requestError"` hook to log errors

```typescript
import { {{$sdkName}} } from "{{.Global.Config.PackageName}}";
import { ProxyAgent } from "undici";
import { HTTPClient } from "{{.Global.Config.PackageName}}/lib/http";

const dispatcher = new ProxyAgent("http://proxy.example.com:8080");

const httpClient = new HTTPClient({
  // 'fetcher' takes a function that has the same signature as native 'fetch'.
  fetcher: (input, init) =>
    // 'dispatcher' is specific to undici and not part of the standard Fetch API.
    fetch(input, { ...init, dispatcher } as RequestInit),
});

httpClient.addHook("beforeRequest", (request) => {
  const nextRequest = new Request(request, {
    signal: request.signal || AbortSignal.timeout(5000)
  });

  nextRequest.headers.set("x-custom-header", "custom value");

  return nextRequest;
});

httpClient.addHook("requestError", (error, request) => {
  console.group("Request Error");
  console.log("Reason:", `${error}`);
  console.log("Endpoint:", `${request.method} ${request.url}`);
  console.groupEnd();
});

const sdk = new {{$sdkName}}({ {{constants.httpClient}}: httpClient });
```